WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:05.850
I interviewed the wall Wolf of wall street
Jordan Belfort earlier this week.

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:10.580
And one of the things that came up was Jordan
saying, I don't care what you do, I'm socially

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:15.090
liberal, but I'm fiscally conservative and
this actually makes no sense and I want to

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:17.080
discuss that with you today.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:22.140
Very often the way that we hear about someone
being socially liberal and fiscally conservative

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:27.240
is they'll say something like, and I have
many friends who say this, I want low regulation,

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.329
I want low taxes.

00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:31.599
I don't care who gets married.

00:00:31.599 --> 00:00:36.190
And I think women should be allowed to choose
abortion if it's what's right for them.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:38.370
Oh, it sounds plausibly coherent.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.059
You're socially liberal.

00:00:40.059 --> 00:00:41.640
And you're fiscally conservative.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:49.719
Well, the problem is if you're fiscally conservative,
you almost always have a position that is

00:00:49.719 --> 00:00:53.719
terrible for the environment and for dealing
with climate change.

00:00:53.719 --> 00:01:00.519
You might have social liberal, fiscal conservative,
self-described who agree that the science

00:01:00.519 --> 00:01:02.059
is right on climate change.

00:01:02.059 --> 00:01:04.710
They accept the science I think is better
said.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:09.610
But if you're economically or fiscally conservative,
you're not going to deal with climate change

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:13.640
almost by the very nature of what it means
to be fiscally conservative.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.420
You need regulation to deal with climate change.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:23.060
Fiscal conservatism is against regulation
under the guise of we're for small government

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:24.440
and it's a direct contradiction.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:25.800
It doesn't make sense.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:32.080
The free market, which fiscal conservatives,
um, uh, pray at the altar of, in many cases

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:34.650
will not solve the climate crisis.

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:41.110
Socially liberal might mean you have empathy
for the poor and you want to fix homelessness

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:44.260
and deal with uninsured Americans who have
no health insurance.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:45.509
Okay, great.

00:01:45.509 --> 00:01:50.250
But the problem is that fiscal conservatism
believes in tax schemes that will not deal

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:54.600
with the poor and with homelessness and getting
everybody healthcare, you can't do it.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:55.920
It's a direct contradiction.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.390
You can say it, but it doesn't make it a logical
thing.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:04.690
If you are fiscally conservative, you often
repeat this idea, we've got to balance the

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:05.690
budget.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:06.690
I'm a deficit Hawk.

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:09.690
We've got to reduce the deficit until a Republican
is president.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.069
Then you often forget about it.

00:02:11.069 --> 00:02:17.920
But if absent any context, your priority is
we've got to cut, cut, cut to reduce the deficit.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.659
You can't deal with the social issues that
you claim to be liberal on.

00:02:20.659 --> 00:02:21.769
You just can't do it.

00:02:21.769 --> 00:02:29.109
So very often when you hear someone say, I'm
a centrist or I'm a social liberal and fiscal

00:02:29.109 --> 00:02:32.989
conservative, you're really dealing with what's
called a right wing libertarian.

00:02:32.989 --> 00:02:37.469
These are essentially right wingers who don't
hate gay people, right?

00:02:37.469 --> 00:02:42.390
I mean, I, I'm, this is a gross oversimplification,
but when I hear socially liberal, fiscally

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:47.709
conservative in practice, I hear I'm a right
winger, but I'm not interested in persecuting

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:48.709
gay people.

00:02:48.709 --> 00:02:53.739
Another way to understand socially liberal
and fiscally conservative is I really would

00:02:53.739 --> 00:03:01.110
love to fix the issues that are affecting
the poor, marginalized, often minority, disenfranchised

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:06.230
individuals, but I don't want the government
to get involved and definitely none of my

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:08.930
tax money should go towards fixing those problems.

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:11.909
It's not a very principled or coherent position.

00:03:11.909 --> 00:03:13.099
That's the reality.

00:03:13.099 --> 00:03:17.780
If you support these social rights, you have
to have an economy that's going to provide

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:19.090
and guarantee those rights.

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:20.730
Otherwise, it's just nonsense.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:25.280
There's a lot of corporate CEOs that want
to be relevant that take this approach.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.180
Sometimes they call it centrism Starbucks
CEO.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:30.519
Howard Schultz is sort of one of those guys.

00:03:30.519 --> 00:03:34.849
He's a social progressive about non-discrimination
in the workplace.

00:03:34.849 --> 00:03:41.079
He's for gay marriage, all of that stuff,
but economically, he's essentially a standard

00:03:41.079 --> 00:03:44.780
neoliberal who is about free markets.

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:49.549
He's suspicious of characatures of socialism
that float around, all of that stuff.

00:03:49.549 --> 00:03:56.109
Now, the other thing I will say is it's a
very small group of people who actually are

00:03:56.109 --> 00:03:58.689
socially liberal and fiscally conservative.

00:03:58.689 --> 00:04:01.690
Not when you say, Hey, where do you fall on
the political spectrum?

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:07.010
When you do a proper poll or test where people
are answering, answering questions about their

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:13.029
beliefs, the number of people that are actually
socially liberal and fiscally conservative

00:04:13.029 --> 00:04:14.400
is very small.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:18.880
You pull people on the issues and you find
in many studies, for example, one done by

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:23.840
the voter study group, uh, whose results were
putting up on the, on the screen, the area

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:27.110
that is least common in this four quadrant
system.

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:32.850
So imagine a four box where you can be socially
and economically liberal.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:37.310
You can be socially and economically conservative
or you can be a mix.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:43.230
The least common quadrant is socially liberal
and economically conservative.

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:49.570
It's actually far more common to be socially
conservative, but economically what we might

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:53.000
call liberal in the more progressive sense.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:59.130
So even many of the people claiming that they
are socially liberal and fiscally conservative

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:00.590
actually aren't.

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:05.760
But the more important analysis is that it's
incoherent if you are socially liberal and

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:06.970
fiscally conservative.

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:12.720
The fiscal conservatism essentially makes
your social liberalness irrelevant beyond

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:14.750
just you don't hate gay people.

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:20.300
And again, it's a, it's a gross over simplification,
but it really illuminates the contradiction.

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:25.690
If you aren't going to be pushing for regulating
businesses in a way that makes sense.

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:32.320
If you're not going to be pushing for economic
justice policies, being socially liberal in

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:40.590
some uh, uh, in, in name only way, I guess
is what I'm saying is not really worth much

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:42.330
in terms of policy.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:44.580
If you disagree with me, let me know.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:47.080
I'm on Twitter at D Pacman.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:49.410
The show is on Twitter at David Pakman show.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:54.900
We are going to take some phone calls coming
up and later on we're going to get back to

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:55.900
this

00:05:55.900 --> 00:06:02.040
issue of the regulation of social networks
and what should and shouldn't be part of that.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:56.110
That's coming up later, but much

00:06:56.110 --> 00:07:58.490
more 
right after this

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:02.250
short break

